WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:03.467
-I'm so proud
of this performance.

00:00:03.467 --> 00:00:05.601
The growth that I've seen
through the competition

00:00:05.601 --> 00:00:07.100
is astronomical.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.367
Watching you come
out of your shell,

00:00:08.367 --> 00:00:09.767
get more comfortable
on this stage,

00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:12.434
you just walk around
with this effortless cool.

00:00:12.434 --> 00:00:14.567
That's awesome.
When you make the song sound

00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:16.767
like how you want them to be,
it's incredible.

00:00:16.767 --> 00:00:20.000
Everybody's arrested in it.
And so kudos to you, man.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.601
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here

00:00:21.601 --> 00:00:23.501
with Chance the Rapper.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:23.501 --> 00:00:25.767
That was a clip
from "The Voice."

00:00:25.767 --> 00:00:28.567
I mean, it strikes me
that the four --

00:00:28.567 --> 00:00:30.367
from watching it,
the four of you have

00:00:30.367 --> 00:00:32.167
a lot of great chemistry
together --

00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:33.901
you, Kelly, Niall, and Blake.

00:00:33.901 --> 00:00:36.033
How do you enjoy it so far?
-I love it. Yeah.

00:00:36.033 --> 00:00:37.667
No, we have -- we have
great chemistry.

00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:38.834
We have just a good time
over there.

00:00:38.834 --> 00:00:41.601
Everybody's very, like, loose.
It's very chill.

00:00:41.601 --> 00:00:44.367
Like, even the staff
and everybody

00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:46.667
that works on the show
is, like, just super cool.

00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:50.100
And I'm -- I've, you know,
I've known Kelly for a while.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:54.300
We've been tight.
Niall, I got to meet recently.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.801
And he was really cool.
And Blake is there.

00:00:56.801 --> 00:00:59.667
And...
[ Laughter ]

00:00:59.667 --> 00:01:01.634
I really do love Blake, though.
He's very...

00:01:01.634 --> 00:01:03.968
[ Laughter ]
He's just so unserious.

00:01:03.968 --> 00:01:05.901
Like, you go there, and you
don't feel like you're at work.

00:01:05.901 --> 00:01:08.167
It's like camp.
-Yes, it's very nice.

00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:10.133
You do something
that I find very refreshing.

00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:11.968
You will sometimes admit

00:01:11.968 --> 00:01:14.133
that you have never
heard a song before,

00:01:14.133 --> 00:01:17.133
especially one, you know,
I think, maybe in genres

00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:19.400
that you didn't grow up
listening to the same way.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.133
Which country is an example?

00:01:21.133 --> 00:01:24.000
And I feel like
I would be embarrassed to say,

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.300
"I don't know that song."
But I really -- For me,

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.133
I enjoy it a great deal
because it gives me then

00:01:28.133 --> 00:01:29.834
the permission to say
that I don't know all the songs.

00:01:29.834 --> 00:01:31.634
-Yeah, no, you should have
no shame

00:01:31.634 --> 00:01:33.334
about not knowing a song.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:33.334 --> 00:01:35.434
Like, I'm going to be
very open every time,

00:01:35.434 --> 00:01:37.534
and it's typically like
it's not just a song

00:01:37.534 --> 00:01:39.601
that I've never heard before
that they have on the show.

00:01:39.601 --> 00:01:43.400
It's like a song that none
of my friends have ever heard,

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:47.634
or no one that I know
has even, like, been around

00:01:47.634 --> 00:01:50.968
that song playing in a car,
driving past.

00:01:50.968 --> 00:01:54.934
It's, like, a completely
separate world from the song.

00:01:54.934 --> 00:01:58.234
So I love the voice
because I get introduced

00:01:58.234 --> 00:01:59.667
to new worlds of music.
-Yeah.

00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:01.234
I also think it's helpful
for people

00:02:01.234 --> 00:02:03.434
to know their worlds of music

00:02:03.434 --> 00:02:05.901
maybe haven't permeated the
culture as much as they think.

00:02:05.901 --> 00:02:07.501
Like, I think it's helpful
to be like, "Oh, right."

00:02:07.501 --> 00:02:10.100
This is -- We always think
that everything we've ever heard

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:11.434
has been heard
by everybody else.

00:02:11.434 --> 00:02:13.534
And it's nice to know,
"Oh, right, there's..."

00:02:13.534 --> 00:02:14.801
I think it just
helps us know like,

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:16.334
"Oh, not everybody has lived
my entire existence

00:02:16.334 --> 00:02:17.501
the same way."
-Yeah.

00:02:17.501 --> 00:02:19.133
And I'm learning new songs,
so it's cool.

00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:20.300
-There you go.
You learn new songs, as well,

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.901
which is really nice.
You auditioned --

00:02:22.901 --> 00:02:25.200
Is this a true story?
When you were in high school,

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.868
you auditioned
for the slam team.

00:02:26.868 --> 00:02:29.133
-I did.
-So, that's slam poetry? Yes?

00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:30.267
-Yeah, that's slam poetry.

00:02:30.267 --> 00:02:31.934
Slam poetry, for y'all
who don't know,

00:02:31.934 --> 00:02:36.033
it's the hyper athletic
form of poetry.

00:02:36.033 --> 00:02:37.601
[ Laughter ]

00:02:37.601 --> 00:02:41.901
A lot more hand movement.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:41.901 --> 00:02:44.267
But I was into slam poetry
when I was growing up.

00:02:44.267 --> 00:02:47.868
That was, like, my first foray
into performing arts.

00:02:47.868 --> 00:02:49.767
And -- That's not true.
I danced before that.

00:02:49.767 --> 00:02:52.567
But in, like, making stuff --
-Whoo!

00:02:52.567 --> 00:02:54.701
-Thank you.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:54.701 --> 00:02:56.133
In the making my own pieces,

00:02:56.133 --> 00:02:58.334
I was really interested
in poetry.

00:02:58.334 --> 00:03:00.567
And so, funny enough, that's,
like, one of the only things

00:03:00.567 --> 00:03:02.567
I've ever auditioned for.
-And did you get it?

00:03:02.567 --> 00:03:05.100
-No. It's so weird.
-What? It is.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:06.467
[ Laughter ]
-It's so weird.

00:03:06.467 --> 00:03:08.701
So it's my high school
slam team,

00:03:08.701 --> 00:03:10.067
and they're like,
"Hey, in order to get

00:03:10.067 --> 00:03:12.167
on the slam team this year,
we're hosting auditions.

00:03:12.167 --> 00:03:15.267
We're gonna do
an after-school open mic.

00:03:15.267 --> 00:03:17.467
And based on your performances
and your content,

00:03:17.467 --> 00:03:19.834
we're deciding who's gonna
be on the slam team.

00:03:19.834 --> 00:03:22.033
And I did not make it
on the slam team.

00:03:22.033 --> 00:03:23.634
But who did make it
on the slam team,

00:03:23.634 --> 00:03:27.434
weirdly enough, was Vic Mensa,
another artist from Chicago.

00:03:27.434 --> 00:03:30.400
Um, and -- but weirdly enough,

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:31.767
as some of you guys didn't clap,
because, you know,

00:03:31.767 --> 00:03:33.601
me and Vic didn't go
to the same high school,

00:03:33.601 --> 00:03:36.601
so him getting onto
the slam team...

00:03:36.601 --> 00:03:37.701
[ Laughter ]

00:03:37.701 --> 00:03:40.300
...was more of, like,
a symbolic win

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:43.400
and, like, a huge
symbolic loss for me.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.133
-So not only did
they not pick you,

00:03:45.133 --> 00:03:47.067
they went outside
to other schools?

00:03:47.067 --> 00:03:50.567
-Another school, and said,
"This guy -- This guy is good."

00:03:50.567 --> 00:03:52.801
[ Laughter ]
And so, needless to say,

00:03:52.801 --> 00:03:54.667
I never auditioned
for another thing,

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:56.567
because that level of failure
was crazy.

00:03:56.567 --> 00:04:00.267
[ Laughter ]
And -- And -- But me and Vic

00:04:00.267 --> 00:04:02.567
ended up going on
to become best friends.

00:04:02.567 --> 00:04:04.167
We recorded a lot
of great music together,

00:04:04.167 --> 00:04:05.400
stuff that's on acid rap,

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:07.234
and now have
a festival together --

00:04:07.234 --> 00:04:09.033
the Black Star Line Festival.
-Alright.

00:04:09.033 --> 00:04:11.300
Well, it all worked out then.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:16.734
I was lucky enough to ask
a fellow Chicagoan, Common,

00:04:16.734 --> 00:04:18.567
a question last time
he was here,

00:04:18.567 --> 00:04:21.634
that Common's grandmother and
your grandmother were friends.

00:04:21.634 --> 00:04:24.200
-Yeah!
He talked about it on the show?

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:25.767
-He talked about it on the show
that you...

00:04:25.767 --> 00:04:28.634
-That's so cool.
-...young pre-fame Chance,

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:30.667
-Tony Jizzle.
-Uh, Tony Jizzle.

00:04:30.667 --> 00:04:33.601
[ Laughter ]

00:04:33.601 --> 00:04:37.367
I can't believe
you made Common's grandmother --

00:04:37.367 --> 00:04:39.267
your grandmother
made Common's grandmother

00:04:39.267 --> 00:04:40.801
say to Common, "Tony Jizzle
wants to talk to you."

00:04:40.801 --> 00:04:42.801
-"Talk to Tony Jizzle"
-Yeah. But he did.

00:04:42.801 --> 00:04:44.334
He got on the phone with you.
-He did.

00:04:44.334 --> 00:04:46.300
So he didn't actually
get on the phone with me.

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:48.801
He left
an answering machine message,

00:04:48.801 --> 00:04:50.834
because I was too busy that day.
I was in high school.

00:04:50.834 --> 00:04:53.167
-Right.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:53.167 --> 00:04:54.834
-And when he called me,
I was obviously busy.

00:04:54.834 --> 00:04:56.601
So he left a voicemail message.

00:04:56.601 --> 00:04:57.934
And I remember,
it was such a big deal.

00:04:57.934 --> 00:04:59.567
My homey Reece, my homey Justin
were there.

00:04:59.567 --> 00:05:01.100
We were walking down
the street,

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:03.133
and I was, like, going through
my answering-machine messages.

00:05:03.133 --> 00:05:04.667
And I had --
And it was really common.

00:05:04.667 --> 00:05:07.300
And he was like, "Man, just
keep doing what you're doing.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.400
Just keep trying."
He's very poetic.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:11.934
He doesn't slam poetry,
but he, like, lay-up poetries,

00:05:11.934 --> 00:05:14.367
because it's smooth.
And like, he like --

00:05:14.367 --> 00:05:16.067
But he gave me some --
[ Laughter ]

00:05:16.067 --> 00:05:18.200
He gave me inspi---
-Finger-roll poetry.

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:20.167
-Yeah, it's like...
[ Laughter ]

00:05:20.167 --> 00:05:22.133
But he gave me
some really inspiring words.

00:05:22.133 --> 00:05:23.634
And I just remember,
I was really bummed out

00:05:23.634 --> 00:05:25.367
because I couldn't figure out
how to call him back,

00:05:25.367 --> 00:05:27.200
'cause I was gonna try
and, like, pitch him

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:29.234
on signing me or something.
I don't know.

00:05:29.234 --> 00:05:30.901
But, yeah, eventually,
I met him in real life,

00:05:30.901 --> 00:05:33.400
and I was like, you know,
"Common,

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:34.701
obviously, I'm a huge fan.
You're a legend.

00:05:34.701 --> 00:05:37.133
You're -- You know,
obviously, Common is Common.

00:05:37.133 --> 00:05:39.634
And I just want to tell you
that when I was a kid,

00:05:39.634 --> 00:05:42.334
your grandmother had you
call my phone

00:05:42.334 --> 00:05:43.467
and leave
an answering-machine message."

00:05:43.467 --> 00:05:46.033
He was like, "Tony Jizzle?!"
[ Laughter ]

00:05:46.033 --> 00:05:50.267
That part --
That part is not true.

00:05:50.267 --> 00:05:51.734
I'm doing it up.
But he did know

00:05:51.734 --> 00:05:53.734
it was me, though.
-That's right. That's wonderful.

00:05:53.734 --> 00:05:55.267
And I do think
it's a reminder to all of us,

00:05:55.267 --> 00:05:58.334
like, when you get --
when a grandmother reaches out

00:05:58.334 --> 00:05:59.601
to your grandmother
and says,

00:05:59.601 --> 00:06:00.934
"You have to call,"
like, always do it,

00:06:00.934 --> 00:06:02.234
because you never know
when that --

00:06:02.234 --> 00:06:03.334
because otherwise
you're walking up to him

00:06:03.334 --> 00:06:04.868
being like,
"You wouldn't call me."

00:06:04.868 --> 00:06:06.767
[ Laughter ]
-But you also --

00:06:06.767 --> 00:06:10.033
Your dad was involved
in Chicago politics,

00:06:10.033 --> 00:06:13.267
and so you got to know --
or not know,

00:06:13.267 --> 00:06:14.868
but you got to meet --
-I got to meet Obama

00:06:14.868 --> 00:06:16.734
when I was a little, little boy.
-Gotcha.

00:06:16.734 --> 00:06:19.400
-Eighth grade.
It was very cool.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:21.367
And then I got to meet him
after I got famous, too.

00:06:21.367 --> 00:06:23.300
-That's pretty good.
Did you tell him

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:25.367
when you were a young man
that you wanted to be a rapper

00:06:25.367 --> 00:06:26.801
one day?
-I did.

00:06:26.801 --> 00:06:29.501
And he famously said --
He replied, "Word."

00:06:29.501 --> 00:06:33.000
[ Laughter ]

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.367
They don't believe in us, man.

00:06:35.367 --> 00:06:37.000
Don't believe in us.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:38.434
It was it was cool, though.

00:06:38.434 --> 00:06:40.767
And then he ended up putting me
on, like, a playlist,

00:06:40.767 --> 00:06:42.834
like, after when I put out
some music

00:06:42.834 --> 00:06:45.634
and I was older
and I had a new name.

00:06:45.634 --> 00:06:48.901
He put me on the playlist.
-Well, well-deserved.

00:06:48.901 --> 00:06:50.234
-Thank you.
-Let me point out,

00:06:50.234 --> 00:06:51.567
well deserved.
He knows how to put together

00:06:51.567 --> 00:06:52.701
a playlist,
among other things.

00:06:52.701 --> 00:06:55.133
Hey, what a delight
to have you here.

00:06:55.133 --> 00:06:56.601
Thanks so much.
Congratulations

00:06:56.601 --> 00:06:58.300
on the anniversary.
-I'm so glad.

00:06:58.300 --> 00:06:59.801
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you, Chance.

00:06:59.801 --> 00:07:01.267
Chance the Rapper.

00:07:01.267 --> 00:07:02.734
"The Voice" airs Mondays
at 8:00 on NBC.

00:07:02.734 --> 00:07:04.901
For more information
on the 10th anniversary

00:07:04.901 --> 00:07:06.234
of "Acid Rap"
and upcoming shows,

00:07:06.234 --> 00:07:08.467
go to chancestuff.com.
We'll be right back.

